---
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import ComponentPreview from "../../../components/ComponentPreview.astro";
import {
  tableInBoxTree,
  tableStickyColumnTree,
  tableStickyHeaderTree,
  tableTree,
  tableDensityTree,
  tablePagerTree,
} from "../../../demos/table";
import { getLocale, useTranslations } from "../../../i18n";
import CodeBlock from "../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import StylingHooks from "../../../components/StylingHooks.astro";

const t = useTranslations(getLocale(Astro.url));

const install = `pnpm add @skryensya/core @skryensya/react`;

---

<Base
  title="Table"
  description="Readable native table with horizontal scroll and sticky rows or columns."
>
  <h1>Table</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Table maintains the native semantics of <code>&lt;table&gt;</code>. The overflow wrapper preserves a readable width per column and scrolls the table instead of compressing it. Header and first sticky column are opt-in and use the same contract on mobile and desktop.
  </p>

  <ComponentPreview tree={tableTree(t)} label="Table" />

  <h2>Uncompressed horizontal scroll</h2>
  <p>
    Within <code>.sk-table-scroll</code>, each cell retains floor <code>--sk-table-cell-min-inline-size</code>; row headers retain a larger one. A table <code>data-layout="fixed"</code> additionally uses <code>--sk-table-fixed-min-inline-size</code>. On narrow screens a scroll appears, not 56px columns or letter-by-letter text. The wrapper supports focus for keyboard scrolling and paints a compact but visible scrollbar.
  </p>

  <h2>Sticky first column</h2>
  <p>
    Use <code>data-sticky-column</code> in HTML or <code>stickyColumn</code> in React. The first cell of each row is frozen and its shadow separates the identifier from the data passing below it. It should be a <code>&lt;th scope="row"&gt;</code>. It does not depend on a breakpoint: it works the same with touch, mouse, zoom or a narrow window.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview tree={tableStickyColumnTree(t)} label="Sticky first column" />

  <h2>Sticky header row</h2>
  <p>
    Use <code>data-sticky-header</code> or <code>stickyHeader</code>. The wrapper gains vertical scroll and a maximum height of <code>20rem</code>, which you can override as in this example. The header is visible on both desktop and mobile; If you combine both modifiers, the corner cell is on top of the two layers.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview tree={tableStickyHeaderTree(t)} label="Sticky header row" />

  <h2>With pagination</h2>
  <p>
    <code>data-sk-table-pager</code> is the vanilla enhancer;
    <code>sk-table-pager</code> is the layout pattern (table + bar). You mark the lines, you leave the
    empty <code>nav</code> and (optional) a status and a
    <a class="sk-link sk-interactive" href="/en/components/select"><code>Select</code></a>
    of page size. The size goes to the beginning of the bar; status and
    <a class="sk-link sk-interactive" href="/en/components/pagination"><code>Pagination</code></a>
    they go together in <code>sk-table-pager__end</code>. Use <code>data-layout="fixed"</code> so that no
    the column widths dance.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview tree={tablePagerTree(t)} label="Table + Pagination" />

  <h2>Inside a Box</h2>
  <p>
    Within the <code>padding</code> of a <code>.sk-box</code>, the <code>.sk-table</code> rounds with the nested element radius (<code>--radius-control</code>) instead of the surface: Box provides the surface and the table is an element within it. This way the radius is tighter, never collapses into a square box, and continues to accompany the <code>data-radius</code> axis.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview tree={tableInBoxTree(t)} label="Table in Box" />

  <h2>Local density</h2>
  <p>
    The scope contract is just <code>data-sk-density-scope</code> and a density value. Here <code>--sk-density-factor</code> is relative to the system; write <code>--sk-density</code> in the same node if you need an absolute value. The numbers are still continuous.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview tree={tableDensityTree(t, 1.2)} label="System wide table · 1.2×" />
  <ComponentPreview tree={tableDensityTree(t, 0.6)} label="Compact table · 0.6× system" />

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={install} label="terminal" />

  <h2>React</h2>
  <p>
    The binding is in <strong>Vanilla |React</strong>. In Vanilla, the HTML with <code>data-sk-*</code> is enough; <code>initComponents</code> (and <code>mountIcons</code>) are loaded by the site.
  </p>
  <StylingHooks groups={[{ file: "components/table.css", probe: "sk-table" }]} />
</Base>

